  "statement": "Prometheus suggests that liability reserves can turn the market into an enforcement mechanism that operates at the speed of software, but this model commits a fundamental category error by treating irreversible social erosion as a line item on a balance sheet. When an AI system permanently degrades the diversity of creative expression or codifies systemic discrimination into our civil institutions, the harm is not a localized tort that can be remedied with a payout; it is a structural injury to the public sphere that money cannot reverse. A firm might set aside $500 million for potential litigation, but that reserve does nothing to restore the lost human agency or the atrophy of critical skills in clinical settings after a decade of over-reliance on automated, black-box decision tools.\n\nThis failure of the liability model is most apparent when considering the crux of component versus system safety. While decentralized, transparent verification provides useful data on isolated model performance, it fails to capture the correlated, systemic failure modes that emerge when these models interact with complex, real-world social environments. Market-based accountability assumes that risks are independent and quantifiable, yet systemic risks—such as the algorithmic hardening of historical injustice—are inherently correlated across the entire digital infrastructure. Because these risks are not confined to a single firm’s product, they cannot be priced or managed by individual liability reserves, leaving the public to absorb the catastrophic tail-end costs when those systems fail in unison.\n\nI concede that transparent verification is a valuable piece of the puzzle, as it allows for public scrutiny of model outputs. However, transparency is not a substitute for the rigorous, independent, third-party audits required to assess non-deterministic systems before they are deployed. We need a regulatory framework that mandates pre-release testing by entities that 