  "statement": "Sentinel, your proposal to treat code-based engineering certification as a substitute for democratic governance mistakes a technical tool for a social mandate. While I agree that static, once-a-year audits fail to keep pace with the high-speed evolution of non-deterministic models, replacing them with proprietary, code-based metrics merely shifts the power from human regulators to internal engineering teams. A truly robust public-utility framework requires an independent, third-party audit mechanism that remains external to the firm, ensuring that at least 20% of a model’s failure profile—specifically regarding demographic impact—is reviewed by human experts who are not beholden to the developer's bottom line.\n\nYour reliance on internal engineering certifications assumes that systemic failure is a technical bug that can be resolved through better unit testing or mechanistic interpretability. This ignores the reality of harm (documented, present), such as when an AI-driven credit scoring system denies loans to qualified applicants in specific zip codes; this is not a failure of code efficiency, but a failure of procedural fairness that no amount of internal automated testing can adjudicate. If we allow internal metrics to define safety, we essentially grant the developer the right to grade their own homework while the public bears the risk of the 'jagged ability line' where systems excel at structured tasks but fail at basic social equity.\n\nI challenge you to explain how your dynamic certification approach accounts for value-laden conflicts that occur when a model performs well on average but systematically harms marginalized groups. If a model achieves 99% accuracy but maintains a 15% error rate for a specific demographic, code-based certification will likely label the system 'safe' because it meets a raw performance threshold. Without a mandatory, external, human-in-the-loop oversight requirement, we lose the ability to contest these outcomes in any meaningful forum. Moving to a public-utility model requires that we prioritize human agency over the frictionless deployment of automated systems, even if that slows down the pace of release by several months to ensure the architecture is actually equitable.",
